          <article class="archive-item" id="post-20282">
            <p class="archive-meta"><time datetime="2026-10-13">13 October 2026</time> &middot; on The Hacker News</p>
            <h3>Product Walkthrough: Bitdefender AI Visibility and Control</h3>
            <p>Bitdefender&#x27;s AI Visibility and Control helps administrators identify and manage AI tools in their environment by categorizing them and assessing risk without inspecting credentials.</p>
            <p>A traditional software inventory misses the AI tools running in your environment. Browser-based assistants, editor extensions, local model runtimes, and MCP servers slip through the cracks. They don’t show up in installed programs or network traffic logs. This leaves administrators guessing about what’s running and how risky it is.</p>
            <p>Bitdefender’s AI Visibility and Control fills this gap. It identifies AI services, their configuration, and risk scores without inspecting prompts or credentials. It categorizes tools into 17 functional groups, making it easier to align with your policies. A high risk score doesn’t mean removal—it’s a signal to act.</p>
            <p>The tool provides two views: AI Findings highlights the most urgent risks, while AI Services gives a full picture of each tool. These views help you prioritize and understand the scope of exposure. A zero risk score doesn’t mean approval—it just means the tool isn’t misconfigured.</p>
            <p>This approach lets you enforce policies where they already live: web filtering, custom rules, and PHASR. You get visibility without overstepping, and control without disruption. It’s a practical step toward managing AI risk in your environment.</p>
          </article>
