            <p>Tamnoon&#x27;s self-healing cloud system emphasizes automated restraint and auditability by evaluating context before acting and logging decisions to ensure secure and defensible remediation.</p>
            <p>The self-healing cloud isn&#x27;t about speed. It&#x27;s about control. Tamnoon&#x27;s approach emphasizes restraint, not just automation. A machine that can&#x27;t say no isn&#x27;t automation—it&#x27;s a liability. Their engine evaluates dependencies, ownership, and recent changes before acting. It can decline a fix with a written justification or escalate when needed. This automated restraint is the foundation of a defensible, auditable system.</p>
            <p>A self-healing cloud closes exposures through the customer&#x27;s own change process, with a clear record of how. Every closure or refusal is logged, creating a durable audit trail. The same misconfigurations keep returning unless the engine understands the context. By learning from past decisions, it avoids repeating mistakes. This isn&#x27;t just about fixing issues—it&#x27;s about ensuring they stay fixed.</p>
            <p>Tamnoon&#x27;s model aligns with agentic AI principles. It builds autonomy per finding class, not blanket permissions. The engine earns write access based on evidence, not assumption. Customers retain veto power, ensuring control remains with the human. This balance between automation and oversight is critical for security, compliance, and governance. It&#x27;s not just about closing holes—it&#x27;s about knowing when not to act.</p>
            <p>The Cloud Remediation Index shows how slowly the cloud heals. Fifty-three percent of all detected issues remain open, averaging 150 days to close. This highlights the need for systems that can act without human intervention, while still being auditable. Tamnoon&#x27;s focus on refusal and record-keeping is a step toward that future. It&#x27;s not about fixing everything—it&#x27;s about fixing the right things, with the right justification.</p>
